[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Its reports say three. The desk merged
them on subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)), and the same subject
comes in for different bugs. Each problem now has its own ticket, with the reports it covers.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix closes one of the three. The other two stay broken after it ships.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} issues={{
  parser: 'https://github.com/rohitpmore/support-kb-4471/issues/1',
  hint: 'https://github.com/rohitpmore/support-kb-4471/issues/2',
  'one-day': 'https://github.com/rohitpmore/support-kb-4471/issues/3',
}} />

Counted the desk's way, as `CauseTable` counts the export: each report once (the export repeats some
ids), under the cause its tenant points to. The one-day reports keep their place under `parser` and
also get their own row and ticket.

## `parser`: the shared parser reads day-first dates month first

A tenant whose locale writes the day first (every tenant on file) sends a date like `07/04`, and the
parser reads 4 July. The report shows a day/month swap: "Booked for 7 April. The invite says 4 July."
It comes in through the app, by email and through booking-v2's form, whose hint says `DD/MM/YYYY`.
This is the bug #4471 describes, and the fix that is queued.

## `hint`: booking-v3's form tells day-first tenants to type month first

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. Day-first tenants on booking-v3 (Alder Physio,
Copper Studio, Pale Harbour) follow the hint, type the month first, and the form reads the day first.
The report looks like a parser swap, but only reports sent through that form have it. A parser fix
does not touch it: the hint must match the tenant's locale, or the form must read what the hint asks for.

## `one-day`: dates move by one day, not by a swap

These reports describe a date one day off, or two places seeing two different days for the same
booking: "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday." A day/month swap cannot do
that. It points to a time-zone or daylight-saving bug in how invites are sent. The desk had already
listed these reports as read differently (`desk-notes.json`) and counts them under `parser` by tenant.
They stay counted there; their ticket is their own.

## How to tell them apart

- Look the tenant up, not the reporter's browser. Intake's tag comes from the browser locale and is
  wrong for many of these reports.
- Booking-v3 tenant, sent through the form: `hint`. Any other day-first tenant: `parser`.
- Read the body before you merge. A one-day move is `one-day`, whatever the tenant.

## Lessons

- A subject line names the symptom, not the cause. Merge on cause.
- A footnote is not a ticket. When the body disagrees with the tag, open one.

